{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794659306137,"sessionID":"ses_e25fd45d9ffevqO0j4AcG7O1MF","part":{"id":"prt_1da07ca99001glTZEWW0OXYhno","sessionID":"ses_e25fd45d9ffevqO0j4AcG7O1MF","messageID":"msg_1da07c4760012qVwk0RKKV0h4H","type":"step-start","snapshot":"9f99630a2b125550a8fc7e9c748ff626c7fa726a"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794659316627,"sessionID":"ses_e25fd45d9ffevqO0j4AcG7O1MF","part":{"partID":"prt_1da07d85f001PiaWT2F23T3qcz","sessionID":"ses_e25fd45d9ffevqO0j4AcG7O1MF","messageID":"msg_1da07c4760012qVwk0RKKV0h4H","type":"tool","id":"a90xi38uj","tool":"write","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"content":"# Iteration 1: Angle 1 — headless dispatch contract\n\n## Focus\n\nHow Vibe runs one task non-interactively: prompt as argument, stdin or file; which flags set model, agent profile, permissions, working directory and turn or time limits; what lands on stdout and stderr; whether there is machine-readable output; and the exit codes on success, provider error, denied tool and timeout.\n\nSelected interpretation: the *dispatch surface a governed `cli-mistral-vibe` mode would call*, not the interactive TUI. Deferred: the TUI keybinding surface, which no dispatch path uses.\n\n## Actions Taken\n\n1. Read the captured headless contract `research/evidence/vibe-help.txt` (version, `vibe --help`, `vibe-acp --help`, `vibe-app-server --help`).\n2. Located the installed runtime and read its entrypoint: the `vibe` console script execs a bundled Rust binary, so the Python package is the app-server/ACP engine, not the headless renderer.\n3. Read the app server's run-outcome taxonomy (`run_export.py`) for the exit-code contract and export shape.\n4. Read the protocol launch surface (`AgentConfig` in `app_server/protocol.py`) that the Rust client fills.\n5. Ran one live smoke dispatch (`vibe -p \"Reply with exactly OK\" --output json --max-turns 1`) with `VIBE_HOME` redirected into the lineage and the workdir set to a lineage-local scratch directory, stdin closed. It exited 1 on a missing API key, which confirms the exit-1 branch and the stderr shape without spending tokens.\n\n## Findings\n\n1. Headless mode is `-p/--prompt [<PROMPT>]`: the prompt is taken from the flag argument, from stdin when the flag carries no value, or from `--prompt-file <PATH>`. `--workdir <CWD>` (env `VIBE_CWD`) sets the working directory, and `--output` selects `text` (default), `json` or `streaming`. [SOURCE: specs/cli-external-orchestration/runtime-lifecycle/004-cli-mistral-vibe-creation/001-deep-research/research/evidence/vibe-help.txt:28-31]\n\n2. Four independent limits exist and all are headless-only: `--max-turns` (assistant turns), `--max-price` (cumulative dollars across the root session *and* subagents), `--max-tokens` (cumulative prompt + completion tokens), and `--time-limit <SECONDS>`, which interrupts the run, writes the export and exits 3. [SOURCE: specs/cli-external-orchestration/runtime-lifecycle/004-cli-mistral-vibe-creation/001-deep-research/research/evidence/vibe-help.txt:32-39]\n\n3. The remaining dispatch knobs are: `--agent <NAME>` (builtin `ask`, `accept-edits`, `smart-approve`, `auto-approve`, or a custom TOML under `~/.vibe/agents/NAME.toml`), `--model <ALIAS>` (headless only, new sessions only, not persisted), `--auto-approve`/`--yolo`, `--smart-approve`, `--enabled-tools`/`--disabled-tools` (repeatable; in headless, `--enabled-tools` disables all others), `--trust`, `--add-dir`, `--worktree`, `--system-prompt-file`, and `--output-dir`. [SOURCE: specs/cli-external-orchestration/runtime-lifecycle/004-cli-mistral-vibe-creation/001-deep-research/research/evidence/vibe-help.txt:44-65]\n\n4. The exit-code contract is explicit and closed: `0` finished; `1` usage_error or config_error; `2` infrastructure_failure (model API after retries, or the runtime); `3` turn_limit, token_limit, price_limit, deadline, terminated, length or refusal; `4` aborted by the server on the other end of `--agent-socket`. [SOURCE: /Users/michelkerkmeester/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/app_server/run_export.py:36-80]\n\n5. `--output-dir <DIR>` writes `export.json` on *every* exit — Vibe version, effective config, token usage, cost, stop reason and outcome — plus a copy of the session journal under `session/` and each subagent's under `subagents/<session ID>/`. That is the machine-readable run record a governed dispatch should read instead of parsing stdout. [SOURCE: /Users/michelkerkmeester/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/app_server/run_export.py:1-12]\n\n6. A live unauthenticated dispatch exits `1`, writes nothing to stdout, and puts `Reading API keys from the macOS Keychain (set them as environment variables to skip this)` followed by `Error: Missing MISTRAL_API_KEY environment variable for mistral provider` on stderr. So the failure channel is stderr, and a keychain lookup happens on every dispatch unless the key is supplied as an environment variable. [SOURCE: specs/cli-external-orchestration/runtime-lifecycle/004-cli-mistral-vibe-creation/001-deep-research/research/lineages/mistral-deepseek/scratch/smoke1/stderr.txt:1-2]\n\n7. The `vibe` console script is a thin exec shim: it resolves the bundled `_bin/vibe-rs` (28 MB) and `os.execvpe`s it, so the headless output rendering is Rust-side and not readable from the Python package. The Python package supplies the app server (`vibe-app-server`) and ACP (`vibe-acp`) engines. [SOURCE: /Users/michelkerkmeester/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/cli/launcher.py:1-40]\n\n8. The protocol-level launch surface behind those flags is `AgentConfig`: `headless`, `workdir`/`cwd`, `agent`, `auto_approve`, `enabled_tools`, `disabled_tools`, `max_turns`, `max_price`, `max_session_tokens`, `trust_workspace`, `system_prompt`, `mcp_servers`, plus `tools` (client-provided tool definitions) and `hooks` (client-provided hook definitions). A governed dispatch can therefore set every knob either through flags or through the app-server protocol. [SOURCE: /Users/michelkerkmeester/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/app_server/protocol.py:385-425]\n\n## Ruled Out\n\n- Reading the headless JSON schema out of the Rust binary with `strings`: the serde structs do not leave literal JSON key names in the binary (only two `{\"type\":` fragments), so the exact `--output json` payload shape stays unconfirmed. Replaced by the `export.json` contract, which is Python-side and fully readable.\n- Treating `vibe --help` as the whole contract: it is complete for flags but silent on exit codes and on the export, which only the app server documents.\n\n## Dead Ends\n\n- Extracting the `--output json` / `--output streaming` event schema without an authenticated dispatch. The schema is Rust-side and not mirrored in the Python package. Candidate for the second and last allowed live dispatch, or for an upstream source read.\n\n## Edge Cases\n\n- Ambiguous input: \"machine-readable output\" could mean stdout JSON or `export.json`. Selected both and separated them: stdout format is a flag (`--output`), the durable record is `--output-dir`/`export.json`. Deferred: the exact stdout event schema.\n- Contradictory evidence: none.\n- Missing dependencies: no API key in the environment (`MISTRAL_API_KEY`, `VIBE_API_KEY`, `MISTRAL_VIBE_API_KEY` all unset) and no `~/.vibe/config.toml`, so a successful dispatch could not be exercised. Fallback: the exit-1 path still exercised the real binary and the real stderr channel.\n- Partial success: the flag, limit, export and exit-code contracts are confirmed; the stdout JSON schema and the denied-tool exit behaviour are not. Status is `complete` because the angle's core question is answered from primary sources.\n\n## Sources Consulted\n\n- `specs/cli-external-orchestration/runtime-lifecycle/004-cli-mistral-vibe-creation/001-deep-research/research/evidence/vibe-help.txt`\n- `/Users/michelkerkmeester/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/cli/launcher.py`\n- `/Users/michelkerkmeester/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/app_server/run_export.py`\n- `/Users/michelkerkmeester/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/app_server/protocol.py`\n- `/Users/michelkerkmeester/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/permissions.py`\n- `specs/cli-external-orchestration/runtime-lifecycle/004-cli-mistral-vibe-creation/001-deep-research/research/lineages/mistral-deepseek/scratch/smoke1/stdout.txt`\n- `specs/cli-external-orchestration/runtime-lifecycle/004-cli-mistral-vibe-creation/001-deep-research/research/lineages/mistral-deepseek/scratch/smoke1/stderr.txt`\n\n## Questions Answered\n\n- How does Vibe run one task non-interactively: prompt as argument, stdin or file? — All three: `-p TEXT`, `-p` with stdin, `--prompt-file`.\n- Which flags set model, agent profile, permissions, working directory and turn or time limits, what lands on stdout and stderr, and what are the exit codes? — Answered for flags, streams and exit codes; the stdout JSON schema stays open.\n\n## Questions Remaining\n\n- The exact `--output json` and `--output streaming` payload shapes.\n- Whether a denied tool call changes the process exit code or only returns a denial to the model.\n\n## Assessment\n\n- New information ratio: 1.0\n- Questions addressed: headless dispatch contract (Angle 1)\n- Questions answered: 1 of 8 strategy key questions\n\n## Reflection\n\n- What worked and why: reading the app-server Python instead of the CLI help. The help lists flags; `run_export.py` carries the exit taxonomy and the export contract in one docstring, which is exactly the part a governed executor needs and the part a help screen cannot show.\n- What did not work and why: `strings` on the Rust binary. Serde does not emit literal field names, so binary archaeology cannot recover a JSON schema; only an authenticated run or upstream source can.\n- What I would do differently: check for credentials before choosing a smoke dispatch, so the one live call spent here could have confirmed the JSON schema instead of the error path.\n\n## Recommended Next Focus\n\nAngle 2: providers, models and the subscription — how Vibe authenticates (keychain, `MISTRAL_API_KEY`, browser sign-in, bring-your-own key), whether `deepseek-v4-1-flash-1017` and `zai-glm-5-3-flash` can be pinned, how reasoning effort is set, and which usage counter a Vibe turn draws on. The unauthenticated smoke already established that keychain-or-env is the auth gate, which is the entry point for that angle.\n","path":"specs/cli-external-orchestration/runtime-lifecycle/004-cli-mistral-vibe-creation/001-deep-research/research/lineages/mistral-deepseek/iterations/iteration-001.md"},"output":"Created file successfully: specs/cli-external-orchestration/runtime-lifecycle/004-cli-mistral-vibe-creation/001-deep-research/research/lineages/mistral-deepseek/iterations/iteration-001.md","title":"write","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Created file successfully: specs/cli-external-orchestration/runtime-lifecycle/004-cli-mistral-vibe-creation/001-deep-research/research/lineages/mistral-deepseek/iterations/iteration-001.md"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"content\": \"# Iteration 1: Angle 1 — headless dispatch contract\\n\\n## Focus\\n\\nHow Vibe runs one task non-interactively: prompt as argument, stdin or file; which flags set model, agent profile, permissions, working directory and turn or time limits; what lands on stdout and stderr; whether there is machine-readable output; and the exit codes on success, provider error, denied tool and timeout.\\n\\nSelected interpretation: the *dispatch surface a governed `cli-mistral-vibe` mode would call*, not the interactive TUI. Deferred: the TUI keybinding surface, which no dispatch path uses.\\n\\n## Actions Taken\\n\\n1. Read the captured headless contract `research/evidence/vibe-help.txt` (version, `vibe --help`, `vibe-acp --help`, `vibe-app-server --help`).\\n2. Located the installed runtime and read its entrypoint: the `vibe` console script execs a bundled Rust binary, so the Python package is the app-server/ACP engine, not the headless renderer.\\n3. Read the app server's run-outcome taxonomy (`run_export.py`) for the exit-code contract and export shape.\\n4. Read the protocol launch surface (`AgentConfig` in `app_server/protocol.py`) that the Rust client fills.\\n5. Ran one live smoke dispatch (`vibe -p \\\"Reply with exactly OK\\\" --output json --max-turns 1`) with `VIBE_HOME` redirected into the lineage and the workdir set to a lineage-local scratch directory, stdin closed. It exited 1 on a missing API key, which confirms the exit-1 branch and the stderr shape without spending tokens.\\n\\n## Findings\\n\\n1. Headless mode is `-p/--prompt [<PROMPT>]`: the prompt is taken from the flag argument, from stdin when the flag carries no value, or from `--prompt-file <PATH>`. `--workdir <CWD>` (env `VIBE_CWD`) sets the working directory, and `--output` selects `text` (default), `json` or `streaming`. [SOURCE: specs/cli-external-orchestration/runtime-lifecycle/004-cli-mistral-vibe-creation/001-deep-research/research/evidence/vibe-help.txt:28-31]\\n\\n2. Four independent limits exist and all are headless-only: `--max-turns` (assistant turns), `--max-price` (cumulative dollars across the root session *and* subagents), `--max-tokens` (cumulative prompt + completion tokens), and `--time-limit <SECONDS>`, which interrupts the run, writes the export and exits 3. [SOURCE: specs/cli-external-orchestration/runtime-lifecycle/004-cli-mistral-vibe-creation/001-deep-research/research/evidence/vibe-help.txt:32-39]\\n\\n3. The remaining dispatch knobs are: `--agent <NAME>` (builtin `ask`, `accept-edits`, `smart-approve`, `auto-approve`, or a custom TOML under `~/.vibe/agents/NAME.toml`), `--model <ALIAS>` (headless only, new sessions only, not persisted), `--auto-approve`/`--yolo`, `--smart-approve`, `--enabled-tools`/`--disabled-tools` (repeatable; in headless, `--enabled-tools` disables all others), `--trust`, `--add-dir`, `--worktree`, `--system-prompt-file`, and `--output-dir`. [SOURCE: specs/cli-external-orchestration/runtime-lifecycle/004-cli-mistral-vibe-creation/001-deep-research/research/evidence/vibe-help.txt:44-65]\\n\\n4. The exit-code contract is explicit and closed: `0` finished; `1` usage_error or config_error; `2` infrastructure_failure (model API after retries, or the runtime); `3` turn_limit, token_limit, price_limit, deadline, terminated, length or refusal; `4` aborted by the server on the other end of `--agent-socket`. [SOURCE: /Users/michelkerkmeester/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/app_server/run_export.py:36-80]\\n\\n5. `--output-dir <DIR>` writes `export.json` on *every* exit — Vibe version, effective config, token usage, cost, stop reason and outcome — plus a copy of the session journal under `session/` and each subagent's under `subagents/<session ID>/`. That is the machine-readable run record a governed dispatch should read instead of parsing stdout. [SOURCE: /Users/michelkerkmeester/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/app_server/run_export.py:1-12]\\n\\n6. A live unauthenticated dispatch exits `1`, writes nothing to stdout, and puts `Reading API keys from the macOS Keychain (set them as environment variables to skip this)` followed by `Error: Missing MISTRAL_API_KEY environment variable for mistral provider` on stderr. So the failure channel is stderr, and a keychain lookup happens on every dispatch unless the key is supplied as an environment variable. [SOURCE: specs/cli-external-orchestration/runtime-lifecycle/004-cli-mistral-vibe-creation/001-deep-research/research/lineages/mistral-deepseek/scratch/smoke1/stderr.txt:1-2]\\n\\n7. The `vibe` console script is a thin exec shim: it resolves the bundled `_bin/vibe-rs` (28 MB) and `os.execvpe`s it, so the headless output rendering is Rust-side and not readable from the Python package. The Python package supplies the app server (`vibe-app-server`) and ACP (`vibe-acp`) engines. [SOURCE: /Users/michelkerkmeester/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/cli/launcher.py:1-40]\\n\\n8. The protocol-level launch surface behind those flags is `AgentConfig`: `headless`, `workdir`/`cwd`, `agent`, `auto_approve`, `enabled_tools`, `disabled_tools`, `max_turns`, `max_price`, `max_session_tokens`, `trust_workspace`, `system_prompt`, `mcp_servers`, plus `tools` (client-provided tool definitions) and `hooks` (client-provided hook definitions). A governed dispatch can therefore set every knob either through flags or through the app-server protocol. [SOURCE: /Users/michelkerkmeester/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/app_server/protocol.py:385-425]\\n\\n## Ruled Out\\n\\n- Reading the headless JSON schema out of the Rust binary with `strings`: the serde structs do not leave literal JSON key names in the binary (only two `{\\\"type\\\":` fragments), so the exact `--output json` payload shape stays unconfirmed. Replaced by the `export.json` contract, which is Python-side and fully readable.\\n- Treating `vibe --help` as the whole contract: it is complete for flags but silent on exit codes and on the export, which only the app server documents.\\n\\n## Dead Ends\\n\\n- Extracting the `--output json` / `--output streaming` event schema without an authenticated dispatch. The schema is Rust-side and not mirrored in the Python package. Candidate for the second and last allowed live dispatch, or for an upstream source read.\\n\\n## Edge Cases\\n\\n- Ambiguous input: \\\"machine-readable output\\\" could mean stdout JSON or `export.json`. Selected both and separated them: stdout format is a flag (`--output`), the durable record is `--output-dir`/`export.json`. Deferred: the exact stdout event schema.\\n- Contradictory evidence: none.\\n- Missing dependencies: no API key in the environment (`MISTRAL_API_KEY`, `VIBE_API_KEY`, `MISTRAL_VIBE_API_KEY` all unset) and no `~/.vibe/config.toml`, so a successful dispatch could not be exercised. Fallback: the exit-1 path still exercised the real binary and the real stderr channel.\\n- Partial success: the flag, limit, export and exit-code contracts are confirmed; the stdout JSON schema and the denied-tool exit behaviour are not. Status is `complete` because the angle's core question is answered from primary sources.\\n\\n## Sources Consulted\\n\\n- `specs/cli-external-orchestration/runtime-lifecycle/004-cli-mistral-vibe-creation/001-deep-research/research/evidence/vibe-help.txt`\\n- `/Users/michelkerkmeester/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/cli/launcher.py`\\n- `/Users/michelkerkmeester/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/app_server/run_export.py`\\n- `/Users/michelkerkmeester/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/app_server/protocol.py`\\n- `/Users/michelkerkmeester/.local/share/uv/tools/mistral-vibe/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vibe/permissions.py`\\n- `specs/cli-external-orchestration/runtime-lifecycle/004-cli-mistral-vibe-creation/001-deep-research/research/lineages/mistral-deepseek/scratch/smoke1/stdout.txt`\\n- `specs/cli-external-orchestration/runtime-lifecycle/004-cli-mistral-vibe-creation/001-deep-research/research/lineages/mistral-deepseek/scratch/smoke1/stderr.txt`\\n\\n## Questions Answered\\n\\n- How does Vibe run one task non-interactively: prompt as argument, stdin or file? — All three: `-p TEXT`, `-p` with stdin, `--prompt-file`.\\n- Which flags set model, agent profile, permissions, working directory and turn or time limits, what lands on stdout and stderr, and what are the exit codes? — Answered for flags, streams and exit codes; the stdout JSON schema stays open.\\n\\n## Questions Remaining\\n\\n- The exact `--output json` and `--output streaming` payload shapes.\\n- Whether a denied tool call changes the process exit code or only returns a denial to the model.\\n\\n## Assessment\\n\\n- New information ratio: 1.0\\n- Questions addressed: headless dispatch contract (Angle 1)\\n- Questions answered: 1 of 8 strategy key questions\\n\\n## Reflection\\n\\n- What worked and why: reading the app-server Python instead of the CLI help. The help lists flags; `run_export.py` carries the exit taxonomy and the export contract in one docstring, which is exactly the part a governed executor needs and the part a help screen cannot show.\\n- What did not work and why: `strings` on the Rust binary. Serde does not emit literal field names, so binary archaeology cannot recover a JSON schema; only an authenticated run or upstream source can.\\n- What I would do differently: check for credentials before choosing a smoke dispatch, so the one live call spent here could have confirmed the JSON schema instead of the error path.\\n\\n## Recommended Next Focus\\n\\nAngle 2: providers, models and the subscription — how Vibe authenticates (keychain, `MISTRAL_API_KEY`, browser sign-in, bring-your-own key), whether `deepseek-v4-1-flash-1017` and `zai-glm-5-3-flash` can be pinned, how reasoning effort is set, and which usage counter a Vibe turn draws on. The unauthenticated smoke already established that keychain-or-env is the auth gate, which is the entry point for that angle.\\n\", \"path\": \"specs/cli-external-orchestration/runtime-lifecycle/004-cli-mistral-vibe-creation/001-deep-research/research/lineages/mistral-deepseek/iterations/iteration-001.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794659309663,"end":1794659316627}}}}
